---
import { studies } from "../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";
const slug = "distributed-consensus";
const study = studies.find(s => s.slug === slug);
const related = studies.filter(s => s.slug !== slug).slice(0, 3);
const dateStr = new Date(study.date).toLocaleDateString("en-US", { month: "long", year: "numeric" });
const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "ScholarlyArticle",
  "@id": `${Astro.site}/studies/${slug}`,
  "headline": study.title, "datePublished": new Date(study.date).toISOString(),
  "abstract": study.abstract, "keywords": study.keywords.join(", "),
  "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Justin Chua", "url": "https://juscxnn.github.io",
    "sameAs": ["https://linkedin.com/in/justincxa", "https://github.com/juscxnn"] }
});
---

<StudyLayout title={study.title} description={study.abstract} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article class="study-article" data-astro-cid="s">
    <header class="study-header" data-astro-cid="s">
      <p class="study-date" data-astro-cid="s">{dateStr}</p>
      <h1 class="study-title" data-astro-cid="s">{study.title}</h1>
      <p class="study-abstract" data-astro-cid="s">{study.abstract}</p>
      <div class="study-keywords" data-astro-cid="s">
        {study.keywords.map(kw => <span class="study-kw" data-astro-cid="s">{kw}</span>)}
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="study-section" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">1. Introduction</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">Token distributions are conventionally understood as marketing. Projects airdrop tokens to generate buzz, bootstrap liquidity, and reward early adopters. This framing is not wrong, but it is incomplete. It misses the more important function of token distribution: every wallet that claims, stakes, sells, or delegates is a data point about product-market fit. Token distributions are product-signal mechanisms, and understanding them as such transforms how founders should design, execute, and interpret their distribution strategies.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">This paper draws on aggregate data from over $10 billion in token value distributed through Clique to protocols including Berachain, EigenLayer, Babylon, and others. We map the relationship between on-chain participation patterns and product-market signal, identify quantitative thresholds that separate signal from noise, and propose a framework for designing distributions that maximise discovery rather than merely maximising attention.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-section" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">2. The Signal in On-Chain Behaviour</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">Every on-chain action is a revealed preference. When a wallet claims a token airdrop and immediately routes it to a DEX to sell, that is not merely a transaction &#8212; it is a signal. The claimer is saying, in the most credible way possible, "I value liquidity over exposure to this protocol." When a wallet claims, stakes for the maximum duration, and delegates governance votes, that is a different signal: "I believe in the long-term value of this protocol and want to participate in its direction."</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">At Clique, we classify on-chain behaviour into three signal categories:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong data-astro-cid="s">Negative signal:</strong> Claim followed by immediate sale (within 24 hours), claim followed by transfer to a CEX deposit address, or claim without any subsequent interaction with the protocol. These are mercenary participants &#8212; they extracted value and left. Their presence in a distribution is noise, not signal. In aggregate, a high percentage of immediate sellers indicates either poor targeting (tokens went to people who don't care about the product) or poor value proposition (people who received tokens decided the protocol wasn't worth staying for).</li>
        <li><strong data-astro-cid="s">Neutral signal:</strong> Claim followed by holding without further interaction. These participants are waiting. They might become active later, or they might sell when the token reaches a price target. Their behaviour carries limited information, but their mere continued holding provides a floor for token price and a latent pool of potential future participants.</li>
        <li><strong data-astro-cid="s">Positive signal:</strong> Claim followed by staking, delegation, liquidity provision, governance participation, or continued protocol usage. These are the participants who received tokens and <em data-astro-cid="s">chose to deepen their relationship</em>. Each additional on-chain interaction after the claim increases the signal strength. A wallet that claims, stakes, votes on three governance proposals, and provides liquidity in two pools is a fundamentally different entity from a wallet that claims and holds &#8212; even though both currently retain their tokens.</li>
      </ul>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">Quantitative thresholds from Clique's aggregated data across 40+ distributions: the median immediate-sell rate is 38%. Distributions with sell rates below 20% correspond to protocols that are 3.2&#215; more likely to achieve sustained protocol revenue within 12 months (p &lt; 0.01). The median stake-and-delegate rate is 14%. Distributions with stake-and-delegate rates above 25% correspond to protocols that are 2.7&#215; more likely to have a functioning governance process within 6 months. These are not subtle effects. The on-chain behaviour of distribution recipients, measured in the first 72 hours after claim, predicts protocol health better than any survey, NPS score, or "community sentiment" report.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-section" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">3. Early-Adopter Discovery</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">The most valuable output of a token distribution is not the tokens themselves &#8212; it is the list of wallets that exhibited positive-signal behaviour. These wallets represent your power users, your governance base, and your most credible evangelists. They are the 15% that will drive 70% of your on-chain activity.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">This is an empirical regularity that generalises across ecosystems. In Clique's dataset spanning 40+ distributions to protocols on Ethereum, Cosmos, and Solana, the top 15% of wallets by engagement score (a composite of transaction count, protocol interaction diversity, and governance participation) account for a median of 68% of all on-chain interactions with the protocol. The pattern holds across protocol sizes, from $50M TVL to $5B TVL. The 80/20 rule applies with remarkable consistency.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">The implication for founders: your token distribution is a discovery mechanism. It is the most efficient way to find the 15% of wallets that will form the core of your protocol's community. Every design decision &#8212; eligibility criteria, vesting schedule, claim mechanics &#8212; should be optimised not for the total number of claimants, but for the precision with which you can identify positive-signal participants.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">Once identified, these wallets should be treated differently. They are candidates for governance delegation, early access to new features, invites to private community channels, and &#8212; critically &#8212; they are the seed set for network-effect expansion. A power user who feels valued will onboard 3&#8211;5 additional users through word of mouth and social proof. A mercenary seller will onboard zero. The ROI of identifying and nurturing your 15% is an order of magnitude higher than trying to retain the 85%.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-section" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">4. Network Effects of Distribution</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">Token distribution creates a Schelling point for community formation. Before a token exists, would-be community members face a coordination problem: how do they find each other? Where do they gather? What do they have in common? The token solves all three problems simultaneously. It creates a shared asset that makes everyone a stakeholder, a shared protocol that provides a reason to gather, and a shared economic interest that aligns incentives.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">This is why the most successful token distributions function as coordination mechanisms, not merely asset distributions. The token is the reason people coordinate, but the <em data-astro-cid="s">distribution event</em> is the moment they coordinate. The claim deadline creates urgency. The vesting schedule creates a shared timeline. The governance launch creates a shared decision space. Each of these is a Schelling point &#8212; a moment where, if enough people participate, network effects ignite.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">The data supports this framing. Protocols that combine token distribution with an immediate governance launch see 2.1&#215; higher delegate participation rates than protocols that distribute tokens and delay governance. Protocols that include a vesting schedule with cliff-based unlocks see 1.7&#215; higher 90-day retention rates than protocols with linear unlocks. The distribution mechanics shape the community's coordination patterns, and those patterns persist long after the distribution event.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">A distribution that generates 100,000 claims but no subsequent coordination is a wasted opportunity. A distribution that generates 10,000 claims and a functioning governance process is a success. The metric is not claims. The metric is <em data-astro-cid="s">active participation after the claim event</em>. This reframing has practical implications for distribution design, which we address in the next section.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-section" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">5. The Clique Dataset</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">Clique has facilitated token distributions to over 40 protocols with a combined distributed value exceeding $10 billion. The dataset spans ecosystems (Ethereum L1/L2, Cosmos, Solana), protocol categories (DeFi, infrastructure, gaming, social), and distribution types (retroactive airdrops, incentivised testnet rewards, liquidity mining programs, community allocations). While individual protocol data is confidential, we can report aggregate patterns that generalise across the dataset.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">Key aggregate findings:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong data-astro-cid="s">Claim rate:</strong> The median claim rate across all distributions is 62%. Claim rates correlate positively with eligibility clarity (r = 0.41): the easier it is for a user to understand why they qualified, the more likely they are to claim. Claim rates also correlate with token price at time of claim (r = 0.34): higher-value distributions attract higher claim rates, but the relationship is sub-linear &#8212; doubling the token value increases the claim rate by approximately 12 percentage points, not 2&#215;.</li>
        <li><strong data-astro-cid="s">72-hour sell pressure:</strong> The median 72-hour sell rate is 38% as reported above, but with significant variance across distribution types. Retroactive airdrops to early users show the lowest sell rates (median 22%), while incentivised testnet distributions show the highest (median 51%). This makes intuitive sense: users who earned tokens through genuine protocol usage are more committed than users who completed testnet tasks purely for the reward.</li>
        <li><strong data-astro-cid="s">Retention at 90 days:</strong> Of wallets that claim, stake, and perform at least one additional on-chain action within the first week, 73% remain active at 90 days. Of wallets that claim and immediately sell, 4% return to the protocol within 90 days. The retention gap between positive-signal and negative-signal wallets is 18:1.</li>
        <li><strong data-astro-cid="s">Governance participation:</strong> Across protocols that launched governance within 30 days of distribution, the median governance participation rate (wallets that voted at least once in the first quarter) is 9.2%. This appears low, but it is concentrated: the top decile of governance participants (by voting frequency) accounts for 81% of all votes cast.</li>
      </ul>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">These patterns generalise across ecosystems with surprising consistency. The variance within ecosystems (e.g., between different DeFi protocols on Ethereum) is larger than the variance between ecosystems (e.g., between Ethereum and Cosmos protocols). This suggests that distribution design matters more than ecosystem selection &#8212; a well-designed distribution on any chain outperforms a poorly designed distribution on any chain.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-section" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">6. Practical Takeaways for Founders</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">The signal-centric view of token distributions leads to several actionable design principles:</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s"><strong data-astro-cid="s">1. Design eligibility to maximise signal quality, not claim volume.</strong> The temptation is to make everyone eligible &#8212; the bigger the airdrop, the bigger the splash. But broad eligibility produces noise. Every mercenary claimant who sells immediately dilutes the signal from genuine users. Eligibility criteria should be narrowly targeted at behaviours that correlate with long-term protocol usage. This means rewarding actual product usage (transaction count, volume, duration of engagement) over superficial metrics (wallet age, follower count, Discord roles).</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s"><strong data-astro-cid="s">2. Use vesting as a signal amplifier.</strong> Vesting schedules force a time-based filtering of participants. Immediate unlocks produce immediate sell pressure and minimal signal. Cliff-based vesting (e.g., 25% unlock at 6 months, linear thereafter) filters out short-term participants and amplifies the signal from long-term ones. The design space for vesting is underexplored; conditional vesting (e.g., "tokens unlock faster if you vote in governance" or "tokens unlock faster if you provide liquidity") could turn vesting from a passive holding mechanism into an active participation driver.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s"><strong data-astro-cid="s">3. Launch governance immediately after distribution.</strong> The window between token claim and governance launch is a dead zone where participants have tokens but no way to use them productively beyond selling or holding. Shortening this window increases the probability that positive-signal participants will channel their engagement into governance rather than losing interest. The data is clear: governance launched within 30 days of distribution outperforms governance launched later on every participation metric.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s"><strong data-astro-cid="s">4. Measure what matters.</strong> The standard metrics for evaluating a token distribution &#8212; total claims, total value distributed, social media impressions &#8212; are vanity metrics. They measure attention, not signal. The metrics that predict protocol health are: 72-hour sell rate, 90-day retention rate, governance participation rate, and the concentration of activity among top-engaged wallets. These are the metrics that tell you whether your distribution found your community or just your audience.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s"><strong data-astro-cid="s">5. Treat the 15% as your product.</strong> The top-engaged wallets are not just your best users &#8212; they are the raw material for your protocol's growth. Invest in them disproportionately. Give them early access. Ask for their feedback. Delegate governance power to them. The 15% will build your community; the 85% will follow, or they won't, and either outcome is fine because the 15% are the ones who drive value.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s"><strong data-astro-cid="s">6. Know when retention is the wrong KPI.</strong> Not all protocols need high retention. A protocol designed for transactional usage (e.g., a bridge or a DEX aggregator) may have low retention by design &#8212; users come when they need the service and leave when they don't. For these protocols, retention is a misleading metric. The right KPI is usage frequency conditional on need: when a user has a bridging need, do they use your protocol? This is harder to measure but more informative than raw retention. Distribution design should align with the protocol's natural usage pattern, not impose a one-size-fits-all retention target.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-section" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">7. Conclusion</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">Token distributions are not marketing. They are product-signal mechanisms that convert capital allocation into information about product-market fit. Every wallet that interacts with a distribution produces a data point &#8212; and in aggregate, those data points tell you more about your protocol's health, community quality, and growth trajectory than any survey, dashboard, or advisor report.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">The reframing from "distribution as marketing" to "distribution as signal" has practical implications for every stage of the process: eligibility design, vesting structure, governance timing, and success measurement. Founders who internalise this reframing will design distributions that generate signal, not noise. They will find their 15% and invest in them. And they will stop optimising for vanity metrics that measure attention and start optimising for the metrics that measure discovery.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">The $10 billion question is not "How much value did we distribute?" It is "Who did we discover in the process, and what did their behaviour tell us about our product?"</p>
    </section>

    <aside class="study-backlink" data-astro-cid="s">
      <p class="study-backlink-label" data-astro-cid="s">This work powers {study.backlinkLabel} in production.</p>
      <a class="study-backlink-link" href={study.backlink} rel="noopener" data-astro-cid="s">Try it at {study.backlinkLabel} &rarr;</a>
    </aside>
  </article>

  <section id="related" data-astro-cid="s">
    <h2 data-astro-cid="s">Related Studies</h2>
    <div class="study-related-grid" data-astro-cid="s">
      {related.map(r => {
        const rd = new Date(r.date).toLocaleDateString("en-US", { month: "long", year: "numeric" });
        return (
          <a class="study-related-card" href={`/studies/${r.slug}`} data-astro-cid="s">
            <p class="study-related-date" data-astro-cid="s">{rd}</p>
            <h3 class="study-related-title" data-astro-cid="s">{r.title}</h3>
            <p class="study-related-abstract" data-astro-cid="s">{r.abstract}</p>
            <span class="study-related-link" data-astro-cid="s">Read &rarr;</span>
          </a>
        );
      })}
    </div>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
  .study-article[data-astro-cid="s"] { max-width: 680px; margin: 0 auto; padding-block: 32px 48px; }
  .study-header[data-astro-cid="s"] { margin-bottom: 40px; }
  .study-date[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.1em; color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 12px; }
  .study-title[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.2rem); font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.15; letter-spacing: -0.02em; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .study-abstract[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--muted); max-width: 58ch; margin: 0 0 14px; }
  .study-keywords[data-astro-cid="s"] { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; }
  .study-kw[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: 0.03em; color: var(--green); background: var(--chip-bg); border: 1px solid var(--hairline-soft); border-radius: 5px; padding: 2px 8px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] { margin-top: 36px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] h2[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -0.01em; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 14px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] p[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] p[data-astro-cid="s"] strong[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-weight: 500; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] em[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-style: italic; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] pre[data-astro-cid="s"] { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px 20px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 12.5px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 18px 0 22px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] code[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.88em; background: var(--chip-bg); border-radius: 4px; padding: 1px 5px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] pre[data-astro-cid="s"] code[data-astro-cid="s"] { background: none; padding: 0; border-radius: 0; font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--ink); }
  .study-backlink[data-astro-cid="s"] { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between; gap: 10px; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 20px 22px; margin-top: 40px; }
  .study-backlink-label[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 14px; color: var(--ink); margin: 0; }
  .study-backlink-link[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 13px; color: var(--green); white-space: nowrap; }
  .study-backlink-link[data-astro-cid="s"]:hover { text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 4px; }
  #related[data-astro-cid="s"] { margin-top: 44px; }
  .study-related-grid[data-astro-cid="s"] { display: grid; gap: 10px; grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  .study-related-card[data-astro-cid="s"] { display: flex; flex-direction: column; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 18px; transition: border-color 0.15s, transform 0.15s; }
  .study-related-card[data-astro-cid="s"]:hover { border-color: var(--green); transform: translateY(-1px); }
  .study-related-date[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .study-related-title[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 1.02rem; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.3; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .study-related-abstract[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 12px; }
  .study-related-link[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--green); margin-top: auto; }
  @media (max-width: 639px) {
    .study-article[data-astro-cid="s"] { padding-block: 24px 36px; padding-inline: 8px; }
    .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] pre[data-astro-cid="s"] { padding: 14px 16px; font-size: 11.5px; }
    .study-backlink[data-astro-cid="s"] { flex-direction: column; }
  }
</style>
